WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:04.470
Few Hollywood films have caused as much controversy
as The Exorcist.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:09.600
Its theatrical release in 1973 terrified and
outraged audiences with a grotesque tale of

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.510
demonic possession, and despite barely escaping
an adult rating, it made history by becoming the

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.570
first horror film to earn a Best Picture Oscar
nomination.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.189
It's still considered one of the scariest
movies ever made, but the intrigue surrounding

00:00:21.189 --> 00:00:23.929
The Exorcist isn't limited to its shock value.

00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:27.949
In fact, some of the most compelling aspects
of the movie come from behind the scenes.

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:31.490
Here are some lesser-known facts about the
classic that's been "turning heads" for over

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:32.700
40 years.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.040
“Damien!”

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.930
Real life basis

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.930
Believe it or not, some of the aspects of
The Exorcist were inspired by real events.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:45.039
The film is based on William Peter Blatty's
novel of the same name, which drew from accounts

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:46.699
of an actual exorcism.

00:00:46.699 --> 00:00:50.609
According to the New York Post, most of the
source material for the book was culled from

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:55.640
the journals of Jesuit priests who documented
an exhausting battery of rituals they performed

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.980
on a young boy named "Roland Doe" in 1949.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.100
These priests claimed to have witnessed the
boy speaking in tongues, mysterious skin markings

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.450
spelling the words "hell" and "evil," the
violent shaking of the boy's mattress, and

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.320
the breaking of hospital restraints and a
priest's nose, to name a few.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:15.040
Some of the claims are admittedly embellished
in Blatty's novel and the film adaptation

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.310
— nobody spewed a bunch of green vomit,
for example — but still, that's pretty creepy.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.600
Church support

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.500
It may come as a surprise that many officials
of the Catholic Church were completely behind

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.780
the film's release.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.820
Not only did it drum up plenty of interest
in Catholic tradition, it highlighted Catholic

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.810
priests as heroes performing a time-honored
ritual, even if that ritual got a little repetitive.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.200
"The power of Christ compels you!

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.020
The power of Christ compels you!

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.030
The power of…”

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:49.700
The story goes that one official even dug
up some classified documents for The Exorcist.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.630
While doing research for the film, director
William Friedkin met with the president of

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.180
the Jesuit Georgetown University, who supposedly
handed him a folder containing eyewitness

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.840
accounts of the 1949 exorcism.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.200
Cursed production

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.670
The production of The Exorcist was riddled
with complications and tragedy.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.690
Early in production, the set depicting the
home of the MacNeill family mysteriously caught

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.230
fire, delaying filming.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:15.420
Even more mysterious: the set for Regan's
room, in which the exorcisms would be filmed,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.670
remained completely undamaged.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.040
Many of the cast and crew experienced tragedies,
too.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.620
Linda Blair's grandmother, Max Von Sydow's
brother, and two of the film's actors passed away

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:28.700
suddenly during the course of production,
and Jason Miller's son was nearly killed in

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.210
a freak motorcycle accident.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.440
And then there were the accidents on set.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.470
Ellen Burstyn sustained a spinal injury while
filming a violent possession scene, which

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.420
reportedly still bothers her today.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:43.060
As the unexpected problems piled up, the production
extended well past its projected deadline.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.430
Friedkin even had the set blessed by a priest.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.660
Maybe he should have gone for an exorcism
while he was at it.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:49.660
Wild release

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:54.100
You'd have to be pretty crazy to send 
threats to a teenage girl, but that's exactly

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.660
what some religious folks did to Linda Blair.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.740
After the film was released in theaters, hate
mail poured in accusing Blair of "glorifying

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:01.740
Satan."

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.640
Warner Bros. had go so far as to hire bodyguards
to protect her after the film was released.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.060
And that's not the only bizarre incident surrounding
the theatrical release of the film.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.390
A man fell and broke his jaw during a screening,
claiming the film's subliminal effects caused

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.390
him to faint.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.410
He sued the studio and they settled out of
court.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:21.069
This was just one of countless reports of
paramedics being called to theaters to treat

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:23.880
terrified moviegoers for hysterics.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.200
Anonymous demon

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.530
The demon that possesses Regan is called Pazuzu,
but that name is never mentioned in the original

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.430
film, despite being in both the book and the
production script.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:37.990
Pazuzu is a real-ish demon — he's based
on a mythological Babylonian figure, a king

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.400
of demon winds, comprised of human and animal
parts.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.010
Father Merrin finds an artifact in the desert
at the beginning of the film that is supposed

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.110
to be a portion of a Pazuzu statue.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:52.280
While the demon is seen, it wouldn't be named
outright until the 1977 sequel The Exorcist

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:53.670
II: The Heretic.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.310
Low-budget effects

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.990
The Exorcist may have been unlike anything
ever seen at the time, but Friedkin had to

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:02.180
rely on some pretty everyday effects to create
the movie.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.610
He reportedly fired a gun before takes to
terrify the cast.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.510
Many scenes were shot in freezing temperatures
to maximize the effect of actors' breath and

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:10.770
discomfort on film.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:11.770
That vomit?

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.170
Thick split pea soup.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:18.030
The sound of Regan projectile vomiting was
produced by a demonic voice actress, who regurgitated

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:20.100
mushy apple and raw egg.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.219
And the creepy sound of Regan's head turning
was nothing more than the sound of a leather

00:04:23.219 --> 00:04:25.780
wallet being twisted in front of a microphone.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.860
It's so simple it's evil.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.939
Not a horror movie

00:04:28.939 --> 00:04:32.550
According to Friedkin, all these years of
terror might have been a case of mistaken

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:33.550
intention.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:34.550
He told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.669
"I thought it was a film about the mystery
of faith...but I didn't set out to make a

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:40.819
horror film...but by now, I have accepted
that it is."

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:44.319
That's a noble point of view, but the Exorcist
sequels and prequels have kept the franchise

00:04:44.319 --> 00:04:47.050
pretty squarely in genre film territory.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:50.040
That's all fine as long we we don't have to
watch anymore vomit geysers.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:57.040
[Laughter]

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:58.979
Thanks for watching!

